/* ===================== OTHER PAGES ===================== */
function viewHow() {
  return `<section class="prose"><div class="eyebrow">How to play</div><h2>Rules, set-up and the AI judge</h2>
    <p>Every game follows the same six steps: choose a game, add players and teams, set the rules, play a round, let the AI judge it, see the results. The host (whoever holds the phone or laptop) runs it all.</p>
    <h3>Football AI Referee</h3><ul><li>No jerseys needed: everyone plays in their everyday clothes. Players in matching outfits are fine.</li><li>Use a bright orange or yellow ball so the AI can pick it out from the grass and white clothes.</li><li>Film from one side, high up if possible, with both goals in shot. Keep the camera still.</li><li>In the AI judge step the app walks you through it: box the two goals, click on each player once so the AI learns their clothes, then click the ball.</li><li>The AI follows players and the ball, finds goals, measures who had the ball longest (that player is MVP), and listens for the whistle. It flags close contact for the host to decide. It never gives a card by itself.</li></ul>
    <h3>Al-Karamah (individual)</h3><ul><li>A serving tray, a Dallah, a few Finjan cups and a bowl of dates, all starting off the tray.</li><li>Film from above. Mark one spot on the tray for each item, and click each item once so the AI learns its colour.</li><li>On GO, the player sets the tray in the serving order you chose. The AI hears the GO beep, sees when each item lands in a spot, and checks the order and time.</li></ul>
    <h3>Dhow Sail Race (teams)</h3><ul><li>Two paper dhows in different colours (red and blue work well), two lanes on a table, a start line and a finish line.</li><li>Film from above or the side with both lines in shot. Mark the lanes and the finish line.</li><li>On GO, sailors blow only. The AI times each boat from the GO beep to the finish, and flags false starts, lane exits and movement next to a boat that may be a hand.</li></ul>
    <h3>Two ways to run the AI judge</h3><p>In the browser (nothing to install), or with <span class="mono">yalla_judge.py</span> on a laptop for long videos. The Python judge uses OpenCV and, if installed, YOLO with ByteTrack tracking. Both sign their report with your match's judge code (HMAC-SHA256). The app checks that signature and the video's fingerprint before it applies any result, so a report can't be edited by hand.</p>
    <div class="row"><button class="btn gold" data-act="new" data-game="football">Play Football</button><button class="btn" data-act="new" data-game="karamah">Play Al-Karamah</button><button class="btn" data-act="new" data-game="dhow">Play Dhow Race</button></div></section>`;
}
